Instruction: Read the following segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is 'financial point of view' the sole consideration when determining whether an Acquisition Proposal is considered Superior?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement Segment:
SECTION 9.5. Enhanced Evaluation Criteria. (a) Notwithstanding any other provisions herein, at any time during the Negotiation Phase, the Company Board may consider a Modification to its Recommendation in favor of a Superior Proposal if, and only if, the following conditions are satisfied: 
   (i) The Board, after comprehensive review and in good faith, shall obtain assessments from the Company's Financial Advisor, its Legal Counsel, and an appointed Industry Expert to evaluate both the quantitative financial metrics and the qualitative strategic impact of the Proposal; 
   (ii) The Company must provide written notice to the Stakeholder Group no later than 10:00 a.m. on the first Business Day following the receipt of a proposed Superior Offer, detailing zero or more non-financial risk factors, including market positioning and regulatory compliance considerations; 
   (iii) The Board is required to engage in a minimum of a three Business Day negotiation period with both the Proposal submitter and the Stakeholder Group, wherein amendments to the Proposal may be discussed and integrated, and any subsequent change in recommendation must incorporate both the financial analysis and the non-financial strategic reviews; 
   (iv) The determination that a Proposal is Superior, and the decision to modify the Recommendation, shall be based on an aggregate evaluation wherein the financial point of view is one important factor among the legal, strategic, and industry-specific considerations. 
(b) For clarity, a Proposal shall not be deemed Superior if its financial merits are isolated from the broader context of industry standards and legal fiduciary duties of the Board.


