Instruction: Read the following excerpt from an Equity Merger Engagement Agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the provisions.

Question: In the context of the Agreement, is the 'financial point of view' the sole consideration when determining whether an Acquisition Proposal is Superior?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Equity Merger Engagement Agreement Excerpt:
Section 9.4. Exclusive Evaluation. Notwithstanding any other provision herein, before the Board may effect any Change of Recommendation pursuant to a Superior Acquisition Proposal, all of the following conditions must be satisfied: 
(1) The Finance Committee must initially ascertain that the Proposal offers competitively priced financial terms;
(2) The Board must engage in consultations with its external legal counsel and a strategic planning advisory firm to evaluate material non-financial benefits or liabilities inherent in the Proposal;
(3) A negotiation period of no less than three (3) Business Days shall be instituted to allow Parent to submit counter-offers that address issues relating to operational synergies, compliance risks, and long-term corporate strategy;
(4) The evaluation must integrate the Company’s fiduciary duties by assessing non-monetary factors, including strategic alignment and market positioning, alongside the quantitative financial aspects; and
(5) Any modification to the operating or strategic terms that could affect the overall outlook of the Proposal mandates a re-assessment of both its financial and qualitative elements.

Based on the conditions outlined above, determine whether the Agreement considers only the financial point of view when evaluating the superiority of an Acquisition Proposal.
